 ## Subtopics
 
-### Comparison with Early Ray Termination
-Early Splat Termination is the splatting equivalent of Early Ray Termination. While Early Ray Termination skips ray samples when opacity is full, Early Splat Termination skips entire voxels. However, Early Splat Termination has higher associated costs per check than Early Ray Termination, making it less suitable for sparse or low-opacity volumes [S3].
+### Early Ray Termination
+A ray-casting optimization that stops ray traversal when accumulated opacity reaches a threshold. It is generally more efficient for empty-space-heavy datasets because the cost per step is lower than splatting's footprint mapping [S3].
+
+### Splat Size Culling
+A sibling optimization that skips splats based on their projected footprint size. It differs from Early Splat Termination by using geometric bounds rather than accumulated opacity [2849-splat-size-culling.md].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** The check step is $O(N_{pixels})$ per voxel, where $N_{pixels}$ is the number of pixels in the splat's bounding box. This adds overhead to every voxel, unlike Early Ray Termination which has minimal overhead [S3].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing an additional opacity volume alongside the density volume [S2, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Effective for dense, opaque regions where splats are occluded. Ineffective for low-opacity datasets (e.g., shockwave) where few pixels reach full opacity, resulting in rendering times significantly higher than ray casting (up to 7x slower in tested cases) [S3].
-- **Strengths:** Reduces redundant blending operations in opaque areas.
-- **Limitations:** High overhead for opacity checks; poor performance on low-opacity or sparse data [S3].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The check is $O(A)$ where $A$ is the area of the bounding box in pixels. In the worst case (no termination), it adds overhead to every voxel. In the best case (full opacity), it saves the cost of splatting.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires an additional screen-space opacity buffer, $O(W \times H)$ where $W, H$ are screen dimensions.
+*   **Measured Performance:** The associated costs for early splat elimination are high, making it less effective than early ray termination for datasets with low opacity or high empty space (e.g., skull datasets) [S3]. It is more beneficial for dense, high-opacity regions [S3].
+*   **Strengths:** Reduces redundant calculations in opaque regions.
+*   **Limitations:** High overhead for sparse or low-opacity data; less effective than ray-casting optimizations in such cases [S3].
 
 ## Background
-The concept was introduced as a speed optimization for interactive volume rendering, drawing direct analogy to early ray termination techniques [S1, S2, unofficial]. It is part of the broader effort to accelerate splatting methods, such as those using shear-warp factorization or EWA resampling, to achieve real-time performance [S1, unofficial]. The method highlights the trade-offs between splatting and ray casting, particularly regarding the cost of footprint mapping versus trilinear interpolation [S3].
+Early Splat Termination was introduced as a speed optimization for volume splatting, drawing direct analogy to early ray termination in ray casting [S2, unofficial]. It is associated with Krüger's work on speed-optimized splatting [S1, unofficial]. The technique addresses the inefficiency of distant voxels affecting the same number of pixels as near voxels, by skipping those that do not contribute to the final image due to occlusion [S2, unofficial].
 
